Now we wait for Lindar to run the program that will automatically generate
the sound files for
the entries, which are all listed in alphabetical order in column 'H' of
page 'items' in document 'tinyurl.com/smartfmgismu', wait for it to finish,
and upload them to 'http://www.lojban.org/tiki/valsi+Sound+Files'.

Seeing as the name of the .mp3 file needs to be the same as the entry
itself, the naming is rather easy, and since the document '
tinyurl.com/smartfmgismu' can be downloaded, it's a rather simple matter to
get the needed entries as well.

I can't do this myself due to not having access to my computer at this
point, but you could probably bash script it. Assuming that Orca is on
Linux, and operates as >orca {input text} {output file} -{options}, the
actual recording execution would be 'orca %1 %1.mp3', with a for loop to
read in each of the column 'H' entries into %1. The hard part, I imagine,
would be extracting column 'H' into a text file.

So, at a guess, maybe 5 minutes to extract 'H', 10 minutes to code the bash
script, 45 minutes to debug and make sure it works correctly. So, my
conservative estimate on the amount of time it would take to record an audio
file for each of the entries is two hours, not including the amount of time
spent leaving the computer on and waiting for the script to finish, or the
time required to upload the files (archived or otherwise) to '
http://www.lojban.org/tiki/valsi+Sound+Files'.
